1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,570
As a child, I was always told to 'eat my greens'.
小时候，我总是被叮嘱“把绿色蔬菜吃掉”。

2
00:00:04,570 --> 00:00:08,800
These were the unappealing vegetables that sat on the edge of my plate.
这些就是放在我盘子边缘那些没有吸引力的蔬菜。

3
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:13,320
Peas, broccoli and green beans, all looked and tasted disgusting.
豌豆、西兰花和四季豆，看起来和吃起来都很令人厌恶。

4
00:00:13,320 --> 00:00:19,750
Let's face it, when there were so many other edible treats to enjoy, why eat boring veg?
让我们面对现实吧，有那么多其他可以食用的美食，为什么要吃枯燥无味的蔬菜呢？

5
00:00:19,750 --> 00:00:27,120
Since then my taste buds have developed and I'm also fully aware of the health benefits of eating fresh vegetables.
从那以后，我的味蕾逐渐发育成熟，而且我也充分意识到吃新鲜蔬菜对健康的益处。

6
00:00:27,120 --> 00:00:32,460
But we still need reminding of the amazing goodness these green superfoods give us.
但我们仍然需要被提醒这些绿色超级食物带给我们的惊人益处。

7
00:00:32,460 --> 00:00:43,940
In the UK, a campaign based on advice from the World Health Organization has been running for several years to encourage us to eat our '5 A Day' - five portions of fruit and vegetables.
在英国，一项基于世界卫生组织建议的运动已经开展了好几年，鼓励我们做到“每日五蔬果”——即五份水果和蔬菜。

8
00:00:43,940 --> 00:00:55,280
That's because evidence has shown there are significant health benefits to getting at least five 80g portions of a variety of fruit and vegetables every day.
这是因为有证据表明，每天至少摄入五份80克不同种类的水果和蔬菜对健康有显著益处。

9
00:00:55,280 --> 00:01:05,500
But I struggle trying to fit these five portions into my daily diet, partly because I have a sweet tooth and vegetables are, well, tasteless.
但我很难将这五份蔬果纳入我的日常饮食中，部分原因是我喜欢吃甜食，而且蔬菜嘛，确实没什么味道。

10
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:10,250
Researchers have been looking at how to make eating vegetables more attractive.
研究人员一直在研究如何让吃蔬菜变得更有吸引力。

11
00:01:10,250 --> 00:01:17,380
They analysed the psychology behind our food choices and found that most of us are motivated by taste.
他们分析了我们选择食物背后的心理，发现我们大多数人都是受味道驱使的。

12
00:01:17,380 --> 00:01:27,200
Brad Turnwald from Stanford University says that "studies show that people tend to think of healthier options as less tasty for some reason."
斯坦福大学的布拉德·特恩瓦尔德说：“研究表明，出于某种原因，人们倾向于认为更健康的选择是更寡淡无味的。”

13
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:37,400
His team carried out an experiment in the university cafeteria where they gave vegetables seductive names and found sales increased by 25 per cent.
他的团队在大学的自助餐厅进行了一项实验，他们给蔬菜起了诱人的名字，结果发现蔬菜的销量增加了25%。

14
00:01:37,400 --> 00:01:50,030
They got rid of healthy labels such as 'wholesome' and gave identical dishes names like 'sizzlin' beans', 'dynamite beets' and 'twisted citrus-glazed carrots'.
他们去掉了像“有益健康的”这样的健康标签，给同样的菜肴起了像“滋滋作响的豆子”、“火爆甜菜”和“扭曲的橙黄釉胡萝卜”这样的名字。

15
00:01:50,030 --> 00:01:55,720
It seems that these indulgent names tempted diners to fill their plates.
似乎这些诱人的名字诱惑用餐者把盘子装满。

16
00:01:55,720 --> 00:02:05,340
Brad Turnwald says that "labels really can influence our sensory experience, affecting how tasty and filling we think food will be."
布拉德·特恩瓦尔德说：“标签确实会影响我们的感官体验，影响我们对食物味道和饱腹感的认知。”

17
00:02:05,340 --> 00:02:12,890
In Europe, a project called VeggieEAT has also been trying to find ways to get people to eat more veg.
在欧洲，一个名为“素食饮食”的项目也一直在试图找到让人们多吃蔬菜的方法。

18
00:02:12,890 --> 00:02:21,550
Project leader, Professor Heather Hartwell believes in "health by stealth", subtly nudging people into eating the right things.
项目负责人希瑟·哈特韦尔教授信奉“偷偷摸摸的健康”，即巧妙地引导人们吃正确的食物。

19
00:02:21,550 --> 00:02:30,820
One idea has been to put a picture of a tasty looking fruit on a supermarket trolley as a hint about buying something from the fruit aisle.
其中一个想法是在超市的手推车上放一张看起来很美味的水果图片，作为从水果区购买东西的暗示。

20
00:02:30,820 --> 00:02:34,090
She says, "Choice is a really complex thing.
她说：“选择是一件非常复杂的事情。

21
00:02:34,090 --> 00:02:40,340
But this study suggests that giving vegetables an indulgent tag can help raise their hierarchy."
但这项研究表明，给蔬菜贴上诱人的标签可以帮助提高它们的等级。”

22
00:02:40,340 --> 00:02:53,020
Certainly, eating 'twisted citrus-glazed carrots' does sound tempting, even if it just tastes like a carrot, but if it makes us eat more vegetables then that can only be a good thing for our health.
当然，吃“扭曲的橙黄釉胡萝卜”听起来确实很诱人，即使它吃起来就像普通胡萝卜一样，但如果这能让我们多吃蔬菜，那对我们的健康肯定是件好事。
